@using BlazorDLR.Shared.Services
@using DLR.Core.Contracts.Rides
@using DLR.Core.Display
@using DLR.Core.Tracks
@inject TimeProvider Clock

@*
	Who is on the ride and what their position is doing (§5.3, §5.4, §5.6) — the list behind
	the "Ride members live" screen.

	It replaces the plain member list that used to sit on the ride's info page. That one
	carried a name and one of three words; this carries the same three words plus the four
	numbers a rider actually asks the app for at the side of a road — how old that fix is,
	how far away they are, how far along the route they have got, and how far back from the
	leader. Two lists of the same people, one of them a strict subset of the other, is a
	screen a rider has to visit twice.

	Every row is derived by MemberRoster, which is where the rules, all four orders and the words
	for the four states live — see that file for why they are not in here. This component is
	layout: a name wearing that rider's marker colour, a state glyph and four figures.

	NeighbourPanel, the other list of live riders, draws the same pill at its own smaller size, so
	the two lists and the map all name a rider the same way.

	**One state per reason, never a shared grey row** (§5.6, §10.1):

	  - Sharing — broadcasting, with a fix fresh enough to believe.
	  - No signal — broadcasting, but nothing recent is arriving. Their last point is still
	    on the map, and it is not moving.
	  - Not sharing — they chose not to. Their pin is not on the map at all.
	  - Private — inside their own private area. Sharing is on, the ride holds no position
	    for them, and their pin is not on anybody else's map until they ride out of it.

	Collapsing any two of those is exactly the ambiguity that gets somebody left behind at a
	junction, so each gets its own glyph — a different shape, not the same shape in a different
	colour, and the word behind it for a screen reader and a long press. The last one also takes
	the four figures away with it — see the note beside them.
*@

<div class="live-members row-list">
	<div class="head">
		<h3>Members (@Members.Count)</h3>

		@*
			The order is a control, not a preference buried somewhere: which question this list
			answers changes several times over a ride — who has gone quiet, who is nearest, who
			is off the front — and each of those is one of these.

			A select rather than four buttons. On a phone it is one tap and the platform's own
			picker, which is a bigger target than anything four buttons could be given in a row
			this width; and the option that cannot work on a ride with no route can say so by
			being disabled rather than by silently doing nothing.
		*@
		<label class="sort">
			<span>Sort by</span>
			<select value="@Sort" @onchange="OnSortChangedAsync" aria-label="Sort travellers by">
				@foreach (MemberSort option in AllSorts)
				{
					<option value="@option" disabled="@(option == MemberSort.AlongRoute && !HasRoute)">
						@MemberRoster.Label(option)
					</option>
				}
			</select>
		</label>
	</div>

	@if (Rows.Count == 0)
	{
		<p class="empty">Nobody on this adventure yet.</p>
	}
	else
	{
		<ul>
			@foreach (MemberRow row in Rows)
			{
				<li class="@(row.IsSelf ? "self" : null)">
					<div class="who">
						<RiderAvatar UserName="@row.UserName" />

						@*
							The name wearing that rider's marker colour (§16.3) — the same pairing the
							map label and the profile preview are drawn with: the rider picks only the
							background, and MarkerColours derives the ink, so a white marker cannot end
							up white on white. It replaces the swatch that used to sit beside the name,
							because one thing carrying both the colour and the words beats a coloured
							square that has to be matched to a name by eye.

							The ink is named once and the border takes it from there — an unqualified
							`border: 1px solid` in the stylesheet resolves to currentColor, which is
							this colour, so stating it twice would be two places to keep in step.

							The full username, not RiderMarker.Label's crop. The map crops because a
							label out on a tile has a fixed width to live in; this is the screen a rider
							opens to read the whole name, and the CSS ellipsis already handles a row too
							narrow for it.

							Italic for the reader's own row, in place of the "you" chip it replaces. It
							is a name the rider already knows, so the slant is a reminder rather than
							the only way to tell — the accent edge down the left of the row is the other.
						*@
						<strong class="name" style="background: @row.Colour; color: @MarkerColours.ForegroundFor(row.Colour);">@row.UserName</strong>

						@if (row.Role != "Rider" && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(row.Role))
						{
							<span class="role">@row.Role</span>
						}
					</div>

					@*
						The presence, as a glyph at the right-hand end of the name line rather than a
						word beside the name. Four chips of four different widths gave a ragged edge
						that had to be read one row at a time; one fixed-width column of shapes is read
						down.

						The word goes with it, because §5.6 is about telling the four states apart and
						a glyph nobody can name does not do that: in the span a screen reader reads,
						and in the key under the list for everybody else. The title is a desktop
						nicety on top of those two rather than the answer for either — a WebView on a
						phone, which is what this screen is written for, has no hover to show it on.
					*@
					<span class="state presence @StateClass(row.Presence)" title="@MemberRoster.Label(row.Presence)">
						<i class="fa fa-fw @StateIcon(row.Presence)" aria-hidden="true"></i>
						<span class="word visually-hidden">@MemberRoster.Label(row.Presence)</span>
					</span>

					@*
						The four figures, each labelled. A bare row of numbers is unreadable on a
						screen this narrow — "340 m" and "4.2 km" are different measurements of
						different things, and which is which cannot be inferred from the order they
						happen to be laid out in.

						A private rider gets none of them, and gets no columns of dashes either
						(§10.1). Every one of the four is derived from a position the ride no longer
						holds, so four em dashes would be four invitations to wonder whether the app
						has lost them — where the glyph at the end of their name line has already given
						the whole answer. Off-route goes with them: a rider who is at home is not off route,
						they are somewhere this ride has agreed not to measure.
					*@
					@if (row.Presence == MemberPresence.Private)
					{
						<p class="hidden-note">Position hidden while they are in their private area.</p>
					}
					else
					{
						<dl class="stats">
							<div class="age">
								<dt>Last fix</dt>
								<dd>@MemberRoster.FormatAge(row.FixAge)</dd>
							</div>
							<div class="range">
								<dt>Range</dt>
								<dd>@MemberRoster.FormatDistance(row.RangeMetres)</dd>
							</div>
							<div class="along">
								<dt>Along</dt>
								<dd>@MemberRoster.FormatDistance(row.AlongMetres)</dd>
							</div>
							<div class="gap">
								<dt>Gap</dt>
								<dd>
									@if (row.IsLeader)
									{
										<span class="leader">leader</span>
									}
									else
									{
										@MemberRoster.FormatDistance(row.GapMetres)
									}
								</dd>
							</div>
						</dl>

						@if (row.OffRoute)
						{
							@* Its own line rather than a fifth figure: it is not a measurement the rider
							   is comparing against the others, it is a thing that has gone wrong. *@
							<p class="off">Off route by @MemberRoster.FormatDistance(row.OffMetres)</p>
						}
					}
				</li>
			}
		</ul>

		@*
			The key. A glyph is worth exactly what the reader can name, and the four that replaced
			§5.6's four words are not glyphs anybody arrives already knowing — so the list says
			what they mean, once, under the rows where it is out of the way of them.

			All four states every time, not only the ones on screen. The question this answers is
			usually asked about somebody who is *not* showing what the rider expected, and a key
			that listed only the states currently in the list would drop the one being looked up
			at exactly the moment it is wanted.
		*@
		<dl class="key">
			@foreach (MemberPresence presence in AllPresences)
			{
				<div>
					<dt class="presence @StateClass(presence)">
						<i class="fa fa-fw @StateIcon(presence)" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					</dt>
					<dd><strong>@MemberRoster.Label(presence)</strong> — @MemberRoster.Describe(presence)</dd>
				</div>
			}
		</dl>
	}
</div>

@code {
	/// <summary>The ride's members (§5.2). The rows are one per member, sharing or not.</summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<RideMemberSummary> Members { get; set; } = [];

	/// <summary>
	/// Everyone's latest fix, keyed by rider (§5.3), or null for a caller that has none — a list
	/// rendered before the snapshot's positions land, or off a ride that carries none.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<Guid, RiderPositionDto>? Positions { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// The planned route riders are projected against (§5.4), the way round this device reads it,
	/// or null when the ride has none. Null empties the two route columns and disables their order.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<TrackPoint>? Route { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// Where the reader is, for the range column, or null when this device does not know — a
	/// browser with nothing shared, or a phone still waiting on its first fix.
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public (double Latitude, double Longitude)? From { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Who is reading, so their own row can say so and the range can start from them.</summary>
	[Parameter] public Guid? SelfUserId { get; set; }

	/// <summary>
	/// This device's own last reading, or null on a host with no receiver (§18.6).
	/// <para>
	/// Only the reader's own row uses it, and only while their private area has emptied it (§10.1):
	/// the circle decides what leaves the phone, and this list is drawn on the phone. See
	/// <c>MemberRoster.Build</c>'s <c>ownFix</c>.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	[Parameter] public LocationFix? OwnFix { get; set; }

	/// <summary>Which of the four orders the list is in.</summary>
	[Parameter] public MemberSort Sort { get; set; } = MemberSort.LastActive;

	/// <summary>Raised when the rider picks a different order, so the page can hold the choice.</summary>
	[Parameter] public EventCallback<MemberSort> SortChanged { get; set; }

	private static readonly MemberSort[] AllSorts =
		[MemberSort.LastActive, MemberSort.Name, MemberSort.AlongRoute, MemberSort.Range];

	/// <summary>
	/// The four states, in the order the key lists them: the two that mean a position is arriving
	/// or has stopped arriving first, then the two that mean there was never going to be one.
	/// </summary>
	private static readonly MemberPresence[] AllPresences =
		[MemberPresence.Sharing, MemberPresence.NoSignal, MemberPresence.NotSharing, MemberPresence.Private];

	private bool HasRoute => Route is { Count: >= 2 };

	/// <summary>
	/// The rows, rebuilt on every render rather than cached against the parameters.
	/// <para>
	/// The ages have to move on their own — the screen holding this ticks a timer at them, and
	/// nothing in the parameter set changes when it does — so a cache keyed on parameters would
	/// freeze the one column that exists to keep counting. The cost is one projection per member
	/// per repaint, which is what the gap list this replaces already spent (§5.4).
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private IReadOnlyList<MemberRow> Rows => MemberRoster.Build(
		Members,
		Positions,
		Route,
		From,
		SelfUserId,
		Clock.GetUtcNow(),
		Sort,
		OwnFix);

	private async Task OnSortChangedAsync(ChangeEventArgs args)
	{
		if (!Enum.TryParse(args.Value?.ToString(), out MemberSort picked))
		{
			return;
		}

		Sort = picked;
		await SortChanged.InvokeAsync(picked);
	}

	/// <summary>
	/// The modifier the row's glyph and the key's are painted by. A CSS class rather than a word,
	/// which is why it stays here while <see cref="MemberRoster.Label(MemberPresence)"/> and its
	/// sentence live in MemberRoster: the rider-facing words are the ride's, the class names are
	/// this markup's.
	/// </summary>
	private static string StateClass(MemberPresence presence) => presence switch
	{
		MemberPresence.NotSharing => "not-sharing",
		MemberPresence.NoSignal => "no-signal",
		MemberPresence.Private => "private",
		_ => "sharing",
	};

	/// <summary>
	/// The glyph for each of §5.6's four states. Four shapes, not one shape in four colours, for
	/// the reason <c>GroupRideLive</c>'s own receiver glyph gives: the list is read through a visor
	/// in daylight and the colour is the first thing to go. The same glyphs that screen uses for
	/// the same facts — a dish for a receiver being heard, a locked house for a private area.
	/// </summary>
	private static string StateIcon(MemberPresence presence) => presence switch
	{
		MemberPresence.NotSharing => "fa-eye-slash",
		MemberPresence.NoSignal => "fa-triangle-exclamation",
		MemberPresence.Private => "fa-house-lock",
		_ => "fa-satellite-dish",
	};
}

<style>
	.live-members .head {
		display: flex;
		align-items: baseline;
		justify-content: space-between;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.live-members .sort {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

		/* 44 px of height, because it is set at the side of a road with gloves on. */
		.live-members .sort select {
			min-height: 44px;
			padding: 0.2rem 0.4rem;
			background: var(--dlr-surface-2);
			color: var(--dlr-fg);
			border: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
			border-radius: 4px;
			font: inherit;
			margin-bottom:8px;
		}

	/* The striped rows come from .row-list; these are the parts that differ. A row here is a
	   block rather than one line, so it wraps its own way rather than being spread end to end. */
	.live-members li {
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: flex-start;
		justify-content: flex-start;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem;
	}

	/* The reader's own row. A left edge rather than a background, so it does not fight the
	   stripes underneath it or the state glyph at the other end. */
	.live-members li.self {
		border-left: 3px solid var(--dlr-accent);
		padding-left: calc(0.6rem - 3px);
	}

	.live-members .who {
		flex: 1 1 10rem;
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		min-width: 0;
	}

		/* The map's rider tag, in HTML: the rider's colour behind the name and the contrasting
		   ink on top and around it. The same pill the profile preview draws, at list size — not
		   pixel-identical to the Skia label and not trying to be, because what it has to get
		   right is the pairing a rider matches the two by. The border is in the ink rather than
		   a fixed grey for the one colour that needs it: white on a light row is a name floating
		   with no tag at all. */
		.live-members .name {
			max-width: 100%;
			padding: 0.1rem 0.55rem;
			border: 1px solid;
			border-radius: 999px;
			overflow: hidden;
			text-overflow: ellipsis;
			white-space: nowrap;
		}

		/* The reader's own name, in place of the "you" chip that used to follow it. */
		.live-members li.self .name {
			font-style: italic;
		}

	.live-members .role {
		font-size: 0.7rem;
		padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
		border-radius: 3px;
		background: #dbeafe;
		color: #1e40af;
	}

	/* The presence glyph, held at the right-hand end of the name line. margin-left is auto
	   rather than a fixed column because .who is the part that grows: the glyph lands in the
	   same place on every row either way, which is the whole point of it being a glyph — four
	   rows are read down one column rather than one row at a time. */
	.live-members .state {
		position: relative;
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		margin: 0.2rem 0 0 auto;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}

		/* The word itself is hidden by app.css's .visually-hidden — see the note there. The class
		   stays on it so this markup still says which span carries the word. */

	/* Colour still separates them, but it is now the second thing that does. On .presence rather
	   than .state so the key's glyphs and the rows' are painted by one rule — a key drawn in
	   different colours from the thing it is a key to is worse than no key. */
	.live-members .presence.sharing { color: var(--dlr-good); }
	.live-members .presence.not-sharing { color: var(--dlr-fg-muted); }
	.live-members .presence.no-signal { color: var(--dlr-error); }
	/* The one of the four with no token behind it — a private area is not a state anything else in
	   the app paints, so a --dlr-* entry would have exactly one reader. */
	.live-members .presence.private { color: #7c3aed; }

	/* The key. Quiet and small: it is read once, by somebody who has just met the glyphs, and
	   then never again by that rider — so it must not compete with the rows above it. */
	.live-members .key {
		margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
		padding-top: 0.5rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--dlr-border);
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

		/* Glyph and sentence on one line, with the glyphs in a column of their own so the four
		   shapes can be compared down the edge rather than hunted for in four paragraphs. */
		.live-members .key > div {
			display: flex;
			align-items: baseline;
			gap: 0.5rem;
			margin: 0.2rem 0;
		}

		.live-members .key dt {
			font-size: 0.9rem;
		}

		/* Only to drop the user agent's list indent — the sentence wraps on its own. */
		.live-members .key dd {
			margin: 0;
		}

	/* Where the four figures would have been. Muted and not red: nothing is wrong, and a warning
	   colour would read as one. */
	.live-members .hidden-note {
		flex: 1 0 100%;
		margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--dlr-fg-muted);
	}

	/* Four labelled figures. Fixed columns rather than a flex row: the numbers change every few
	   seconds, and a layout that reflowed as "9 s" became "10 s" would be unreadable at a glance. */
	.live-members .stats {
		flex: 0 0 auto;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(3.5rem, auto));
		gap: 0 0.75rem;
		margin: 0;
		text-align: right;
	}

		.live-members .stats dt {
			font-size: 0.65rem;
			text-transform: uppercase;
			letter-spacing: 0.04em;
			color: var(--dlr-fg-quiet);
		}

		.live-members .stats dd {
			margin: 0;
			font-size: 0.9rem;
			font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		}

	.live-members .stats .leader { color: var(--dlr-good); font-weight: 600; }

	.live-members .off {
		flex: 1 0 100%;
		margin: 0.15rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: var(--dlr-error);
	}

	/* Below the rail's breakpoint the figures go under the name rather than beside it — four
	   columns and a name on a 320 px screen is four columns of one character each. */
	@@media (max-width: 560px) {
		.live-members .stats {
			flex: 1 0 100%;
			text-align: left;
		}
	}
</style>
